-## Submission and Retrieval
+Revisions are never deleted, so the history of a visit grows with each accepted submission. At roughly 6,000 records per season, retaining every revision of every visit is well within a small database's reach; no pruning policy is defined yet.
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency mechanism | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | The team accepts a larger coordinator review queue in exchange for the server never combining scientifically related edits on its own. A merge would need maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and a rule that pairs a species identification with its cover estimate can combine two observations the team would have wanted to judge side by side. |
+| Acceptance of a submission based on the current revision | Accepted immediately as the new current revision | Hold every submission for review | A submission derived from the current state cannot conflict with it. Reviewing every edit would put every visit in the weekly queue, which the team could not operate. A submission derived from any other revision is held, so no edit is judged by the server on the content of its fields. |
+| Evaluation point of the concurrency rule | A single consistent view of the visit under a visit lock | Evaluate each submission as it arrives | Two submissions naming the same base revision must not both advance the visit. Evaluating under the lock makes the first commit advance the visit and the second a pending revision, so arrival order decides nothing beyond which of two competing edits reached the store first. |
+| Which revisions may serve as a base | Revisions that were accepted, whether current or superseded | Allow a pending revision to serve as a base | An editor cannot sensibly have started from a proposal that was never accepted, and chains of pending proposals would make the review queue harder to reason about. |
+| Scope of a resolution | One resolution closes every pending revision for the visit | Resolve one competing group at a time and leave the rest pending | A visit's pending revisions compete for the same current state, so resolving one group leaves the others competing against a state that has since changed. One review task per visit keeps the queue countable and the outcome unambiguous. |
+| What a resolution may select | One of the visit's pending revisions, as submitted | Also allow a coordinator-authored revision at resolution time | Selecting a submitted revision keeps every current revision worker-authored and keeps the review decision a choice among submitted visits. A coordinator-authored revision is a compatible addition later, because it is an ordinary revision with a base revision, but it needs its own authorship and attribution rules. |
+| Current state while review is open | The last accepted revision | No current state until review closes | The visit's last accepted revision is still the most defensible description of the visit, and review-export excludes unresolved visits from its export set rather than relying on the absence of a current revision. |
+| Plot reference across revisions | Fixed by the visit's first submission | Let any revision re-point the visit | Re-pointing a visit would move a survey record to a different plot and would silently change which permanent plot the observations describe. |
+| Identity of author and coordinator | Taken from the authenticated principal | Taken from the request body | The record store keeps the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable; an author stated by the caller would break that path. |
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Resolved
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+1. The concurrency mechanism is whole-visit revisions: concurrent edits to one visit become competing revisions for a coordinator to resolve, and the server never combines submissions.
+2. A submission that names the current revision advances the visit immediately; every other submission against an accepted revision waits for review.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Deferred
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+1. Tablet-sync design: which revision IDs a tablet retains offline, how it sequences several submissions made while disconnected, and how it presents a submission that the store held for review.
+2. Review-export design: the revision comparison screen, the coordinator queue, and the rules that hold unresolved visits out of the partner's CSV.
+3. Whether a coordinator may resolve by authoring a revision rather than selecting a submitted one. The current contract selects submitted revisions only.
+4. Whether coordinators may also author ordinary visit edits, and how a coordinator-authored edit is distinguished from a worker's.
+5. Long-term retention and pruning of superseded revisions.
+6. Payload size limits beyond the schema's field rules, and the exact canonical form used to compare payloads for idempotency.
+7. How the visit payload schema evolves once species cover estimates gain a richer representation than a number from 0 to 100.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
